The Human-English builds an information system \(\mathbf{D}\) for \(\mathbf{D}\cong\mathbf{A}+(\mathbf{D}\to\mathbf{D})\), with \(\Delta_{\mathbf{D}}=(\Delta,\Delta)\) chosen outside \(D_{\mathbf{A}}\). Clauses (1)–(3) put \(\Delta_{\mathbf{D}}\) into \(D_{\mathbf{D}}\), put an atom copy \((X,\Delta)\) in whenever \(X\in D_{\mathbf{A}}\), and put a function token \((\Delta,(u,v))\) in whenever \(u,v\in\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{D}}\). Consistency is the least class containing (4) \(\emptyset\), closed under (5) insertion of \(\Delta_{\mathbf{D}}\), under (6) the atom copy of a consistent set of \(\mathbf{A}\), and under (7) a finite set of function tokens whose components lie in \(\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{D}}\) and such that every subcollection with a consistent input-union has a consistent output-union, so a consistent set lies in the atom world or in the function world. Entailment is generated by (8) every set entails \(\Delta_{\mathbf{D}}\), (9) insertion of \(\Delta_{\mathbf{D}}\) preserves entailment, (10) the atom copy of \(w\) entails \((W,\Delta)\) whenever \(w\vdash_{\mathbf{A}}W\), and (11) a consistent set of function tokens entails \((\Delta,(u',v'))\) whenever the union of those outputs \(v_i\) for which \(u'\vdash_{\mathbf{D}}u_i\) itself entails \(v'\). Scott records that \(D_{\mathbf{D}}\) and \(\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{D}}\) are mutually recursive, that the information-system axioms and the domain equation are a mechanical check, that a Gödel numbering makes consistency and entailment recursively enumerable and in fact recursive because a derivation for a datum of fixed size has bounded length, and that the \(\lambda\)-calculus operations on this domain are not supplied. `RawLamToken` is inductive, with constructors `bot`, `atom`, and `funTok` of two lists, and `rawLamBEq_eq` decides equality by mutual recursion on tokens and lists, so two lists that carry the same tokens in a different order or with a repeated entry are different tokens, whereas clause (3) uses one consistent set for each of \(u\) and \(v\). `LamWF` accepts `bot` and every atom, and accepts `funTok xs ys` only when `LamConDepth` holds of both `listToFinset` images; `LamDToken` is that subtype. `LamConDepth` recurses on `finsetMaxDepth`: if `lamFunFinset` is empty the finset is consistent exactly when `lamAtomFinset` lies in \(\mathbf{A}\), and if not, the atom finset is empty, every function token has depth-consistent components, and every subcollection with a depth-consistent `lamInputUnion` has a depth-consistent `lamOutputUnion`, which is (6) and (7). `lamAtomInsert` and `lamFunInsert` ignore `bot`, so `LamConDepth_insert_bot` is (5) and `LamConDepth_empty` is (4). `LamEntDepth` has three constructors. The `bot` constructor requires `LamConDepth`, so (8) is stated only for consistent sets. There is no constructor for (9); `LamEntDepth_weaken_insert` derives it once the inserted token is already entailed, because inserting `bot` does not change either projection. The `atom` constructor also requires an empty function finset and a nonempty atom finset, so \(\emptyset\) does not entail \(\mathrm{atom}(\Delta_{\mathbf{A}})\) even though \(\emptyset\vdash_{\mathbf{A}}\Delta_{\mathbf{A}}\) and (10) applies with \(w=\emptyset\). The `funTok` constructor requires consistency of the set, an empty atom finset, a nonempty function finset, consistency of both target lists, a witness subset of `lamFunFinset` whose input tokens are entailed by the target input list and whose `lamOutputUnion` entails every token of the target output list, and the side condition that an atom-world target input forces an atom-world witness input; the nonempty-function premise blocks (11) for the empty set, which is consistent and contributes an empty union of outputs. `lambdaSystem` is the information system on `LamDToken` whose six axioms are `LamDCon.subset` (from `LamConDepth_mono`), `LamDCon.singleton`, `LamDEnt.bot`, `LamDEnt.of_mem` (strong induction on depth in `LamEntDepth_of_mem`: bottom is the bottom clause, an atom uses reflexivity in \(\mathbf{A}\) in the atom world and is impossible in the function world, and a function token uses the singleton witness under the strict bounds `rawDepth_funTok_gt_left` and `rawDepth_funTok_gt_right`), `LamDCon_insert_of_ent` (strong induction in `LamConDepth_insert_of_ent`, splitting a selection that contains the newly entailed function token), and `LamDEnt.trans` (strong induction in `LamEntDepth_trans`: bottom is immediate, an atom is cut in \(\mathbf{A}\), and a function token combines witness subsets by a finite induction, appealing to the inductive hypothesis at a smaller depth on input tokens and on output tokens). `lamUnfold` sends `bot` to the sum bottom (`lamUnfold_bot`), an atom to the left injection (`lamUnfold_atom`), and a function token to the right injection of the two lists coerced to finsets, so distinct list-tokens land on one function-space token. `theorem_82`, which is `lamRhs_eq_sum_function`, is `rfl` identifying `lamRhs` with `sumSystem A (functionSystem (lambdaSystem A) (lambdaSystem A))`. The staged file `lambdaSystem_con_axioms` still leaves entailment-consistency and transitivity of function tokens unproved for `LamToken`, even though the depth system used by `lambdaSystem` proves both. No theorem shows that `LamConDepth` or `LamEntDepth` is decidable. **Theorem:** partial — `lambdaSystem` is an information system whose consistency is the atom world or the function world of clause (7), but function tokens are lists rather than consistent sets, clauses (10) and (11) both refuse the empty set, and `theorem_82` is only the definitional equality of `lamRhs` with \(\mathbf{A}+(\mathbf{D}\to\mathbf{D})\). **Proof:** partial — the six axiom checks are the depth inductions named above, with (9) derived from the fact that `bot` does not change the projections, while the isomorphism, the recursiveness claim, and the \(\lambda\)-model are not proved.
